  "cli_result_text": "Submitted 30 files spanning the three repos, ordered by importance for the refactor plan:\n\n**spring-kafka** (primary implementation, most files): `ConcurrentMessageListenerContainer`, `KafkaMessageListenerContainer`, `AbstractMessageListenerContainer`, `ContainerProperties`, `GenericMessageListenerContainer`/`MessageListenerContainer` interfaces, the `ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactory`/`AbstractKafkaListenerContainerFactory` config layer, endpoint/registry plumbing (`KafkaListenerEndpoint`, `AbstractKafkaListenerEndpoint`, `KafkaListenerEndpointRegistry`/`Registrar`), the `@KafkaListener`/`@EnableKafka` annotation-processing chain, and the existing concurrency test suite (which any refactor must keep green or deliberately break).\n\n**spring-boot** (autoconfiguration consumer): `KafkaAnnotationDrivenConfiguration`, `ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactoryConfigurer`, `KafkaProperties` (owns `spring.kafka.listener.concurrency`), `KafkaAutoConfiguration`, plus their tests.\n\n**spring-framework** (core abstractions the threading model is built on): `TaskExecutor`/`AsyncTaskExecutor`/`SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor` (each concurrent container spins up a `SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor` per delegate) and `ConcurrencyThrottleSupport`, which backs `SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor`'s concurrency-limiting behavior.\n\nThis set is enough to trace the full dependency chain: `KafkaAnnotationDrivenConfiguration` (spring-boot) \u2192 `ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactory` (spring-kafka) \u2192 `ConcurrentMessageListenerContainer` wrapping N `KafkaMessageListenerContainer`s, each driven by a `SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor` (spring-framework) \u2014 confirming spring-framework's role is upstream infrastructure only (no Kafka-specific code to change there), spring-kafka owns the actual threading refactor, and spring-boot only needs to keep its property/factory wiring compatible.",
